nodecode-hackertube is a rewrite of rcmiller01's rcmiller01/hackertube:

  https://github.com/rcmiller01/hackertube
  commit 3e65af3b424bb932de944731340d99122f0a67ed
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Robert Miller (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's search core, as two tools and one command. The
adapters for Hacker News (evidence only, never a media record of its own),
Apple's iTunes Search (shows, episodes, audiobooks), Open Library (books),
LibriVox (audiobooks) and the optional YouTube Data API keep their endpoints,
their bounded per-source request, and their field mapping — including the
details that matter: an Apple show's `trackTimeMillis` is not an episode
duration, a LibriVox 404 is no results and not a source that is down, an Open
Library doc whose key is not a work page is dropped, and a work's identity is
its own id with the source's prefix. The record fields, the format/section
split, the interest gate on title and contributor, the variety-aware ranking
(a community signal far above the whole variety range, so it reorders but
never hides) and the rule that one failed source is named and never hides the
others are the original's, reimplemented in Common Lisp against the cell
contract. The privacy posture is kept: each source's secrets are read only
when that source is enabled, Open Library's contact rides one request's user
agent, and nothing is downloaded but JSON.

Left out: the Hermes dashboard (its page, its catalogue view and its
client-side script), which is that harness's own surface — here the same
search is a tool the model calls and a slash command, and the library is one
JSON file under the home instead of a profile database. Left out with it: the
supervised Python/Hermes CLI worker and its job handles (a search here runs in
the calling process under one timeout per source), the hand-verified
"Recommended in Hacker News" endorsement tier (a story that links a record
counts as a mention, and the block says "linked by", never "recommended"), the
Podcast Index source (it needs request signing, and stays out until it earns
its keep), the 30-day metadata retention sweep and the privacy-policy consent
screen, the stored interests, and the save/dismiss/complete/open state per
item — the library keeps what is saved, and forgets what is forgotten.
